data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider already threw `InvalidOperationException` for sync LINQ operations by default, but you could opt back in (e.g., by suppressing `CosmosEventId.SyncNotSupported`). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** The sync-over-async wrappers for the Cosmos provider are gone—there's no configuration to bring them back.

All four methods will throw at runtime:

| Method | Issue |
|--------|-------|
| `ToList()` | ❌ No sync enumeration |
| `First()` | ❌ No sync enumeration |
| `Count()` | ❌ No sync enumeration |
| `SaveChanges()` | ❌ No sync persist |

## The Fix: Go Fully Async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

This is the only supported path forward for the Cosmos provider in EF Core 11. You'll need to propagate `async/await` up through your call chain (controllers, services, etc.).
